  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 206 AM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nA potent Alberta Clipper including an amplifying upper-level \nshortwave trough will traverse the Mid-Mississippi River Valley on \nWednesday, facilitating the passage of a cold front through the CWA \ntrailing a deepening surface cyclone across the Midwest. As is \ncommonly the case with Alberta Clippers, the majority of associated \nprecipitation will be to the north of the surface cyclone's track \nand north of the CWA, but 40 to 60 percent of ensemble model \nmembership has scattered showers developing along/east of the \nMississippi River as moisture and forcing increase along the cold \nfront. There are still some timing differences, however, that will \ninfluence how far west these showers develop. Steepening lapse rates \nwith mid-level cooling may sufficiently compensate for modest \nmoisture return to enable a few thunderstorms to develop as \ndeterministic model guidance projects a narrow corridor of 500 to \n1000 J/kg of MUCAPE. Additionally, a tightening surface pressure \ngradient will result in occasional wind gusts of 20 to 28 kt on \nWednesday.\n\nBehind the cold front and initial shortwave trough, longwave \ntroughing will amplify across the eastern CONUS with deep cyclonic \nflow ensuing over the Mid-Mississippi River Valley. As multiple \nadditional shortwave troughs and/or vorticity maxima rotate around \nthe parent longwave trough, the pattern appears favorable for \nperiodic light showers or drizzle through Friday. With uncertain \ntiming of each feature and a common dry bias in longer-rage model \nguidance in these patterns, the forecast is currently dry after \nWednesday. Strong and persistent low-level CAA and increased cloud \ncover will at least result in coldest temperatures we have seen so \nfar this season including highs in the 40s to low 50s F and lows in \nthe mid-20s to mid-30s F. Confidence is high in Friday being the \ncoldest day of the period with high temperatures falling to warm out \nof the 40s F, followed by a nearby surface anticyclone favoring \nwidespread low temperatures in the 20s F if clouds clear enough.\n\nNext weekend, global model guidance agrees that mid-level heights \nwill begin rising and the anticyclone will shift to the east of the \nregion as downstream longwave troughing progresses eastward. This \npattern change will lead to a warming trend but there are \nsignificant disparities on how quickly this process takes place, \nwhich determine how quickly temperatures will warm. Accordingly, the \nNBM interquartile temperature range inflates to 15 F by next Sunday, \nalthough even the 25th percentile still demonstrates an underlying \nwarming trend.",
